<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Unitarianism</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Unitarianism is a nontrinitarian movement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> that affirms the unitary nature of God and rejects the doctrine of the Trinity; most Unitarians respect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus Christ</a> as a spiritual and moral teacher, and many as savior, but not as equal to God. It arose in the mid-16th century among the Polish Brethren in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> and in the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, where Ferenc Dávid founded the Unitarian Church of Transylvania. Unitarians faced repression in 17th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and persecution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, where the first Unitarian church was established on Essex Street, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in 1774. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, King's Chapel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> accepted the faith from 1784.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Unitarianism began close to the Radical Reformation, almost simultaneously among the Protestant Polish Brethren in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> and in the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> in the mid-16th century. The Unitarian Church of Transylvania was founded by Ferenc Dávid, who lived from about 1520 to 1579, in 1565 under John II Sigismund Zápolya, and the term Unitarian was first used in 1600. Among its adherents were many Italians who fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and Transylvania to escape persecution. The Racovian Catechism of the Polish Brethren, published in 1605, set out the faith from a Socinian perspective with biblical proof-texts, and the Complanatio of 1638 affirmed infant baptism and the eucharist.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Unitarians hold that the doctrine of the Trinity fails to adhere to strict <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monotheism">monotheism</a>. They regard <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> as a great man and prophet of God, perhaps a supernatural being, but not God himself. In the early 19th century Robert Wallace distinguished three classes of doctrine: Arian, which held that the Logos pre-existed; Socinian, which denied pre-existence but agreed that Jesus should be worshipped; and a strict Unitarianism that denied worship of the man Christ. Unitarians generally reject the Ecumenical Councils and the doctrine of original sin, and hold that reason and science coexist with faith.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the 17th century repression in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> led many Unitarians to flee or be killed, and from the 16th to the 18th centuries Unitarians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, including John Biddle and Theophilus Lindsey, often faced political persecution. The first Unitarian church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> was established in 1774 on Essex Street in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, and King's Chapel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> accepted the faith in 1784, when James Freeman began teaching it; in 1785 he made a revised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-common-prayer">Book of Common Prayer</a> based on Lindsey's work.</p><p>Unitarianism is considered a factor in the decline of classical deism. The movement is a collection of groups that grew at first independently, including the church founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a> in 1795, the first Unitarian church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a> in 1854, and the movement founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> in 1867 by David Faure.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Unitarianism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Church membership was low because it had not kept up with population growth, and Enlightenment rationalism was leading many people towards atheism, Deism, <b>Unitarianism</b> and Universalism.</blockquote><a href="/movement/first-great-awakening">First Great Awakening · Background</a></li></ul></section>
